  "note": "🧠️ The language-neutral law of a windowed container the guest answers SHORT (its item budget ends the run early, from the offset it was asked for): the host learns the capacity from the short answer, places the next window for it — centred on the visible rows when it covers them, else pinned to the edge the reader scrolls towards, the end of the list when the viewport opens there — while still asking the viewport rule's rows, so a settled short window asks the same question again (silence), a capacity learned from an answer to an older, smaller window is corrected by the next answer, and a guest that serves more raises it. Every length is in rows of the container's row extent: `topRows` is the container's top relative to the viewport's content origin (negative = scrolled past), the container is `total` rows tall (no expanded rows), and its materialised rows sit at `topRows + offset + i`. `memory` is what the observer kept from the previous measure (`null` = never measured). Answered in TypeScript by `treeWindowServedRequestsV1` (`💻️os/📺️renderer/🧑‍🎨engine/🧱️elements/🗣️Interpreter`); the live oracle is Chromium's layout under Playwright over a real guest (ticket 26/09/23, S18 item 2).",
